; convert used one scratch amount for the amount, debit/credit and cost
; columns, and balanced each row with whichever it parsed last.  A cost
; column that came before the amount column therefore balanced the row
; against the amount and convert stopped with "Transaction does not
; balance"; a debit column with a cost produced "-10 EUR @@ $12"
; against "$-12", which does not re-read.  Each column now has its own
; value, a cost takes the sign of the amount it prices (as "@@" does in
; a journal), and the balancing posting carries the negated cost.  A
; cost in the amount's own commodity is still dropped.

test -f /dev/null convert test/regress/csv-cost-after-amount.csv --account Assets:Bank
2024/01/01 * Broker
    Expenses:Unknown                          10 EUR @@ $12
    Assets:Bank                                 $-12

2024/01/02 * Exchange
    Expenses:Unknown                           5 EUR
    Assets:Bank
end test

test -f /dev/null convert test/regress/csv-cost-before-amount.csv --account Assets:Bank
2024/01/01 * Broker
    Expenses:Unknown                          10 EUR @@ $12
    Assets:Bank                                 $-12

2024/01/02 * Exchange
    Expenses:Unknown                           5 EUR
    Assets:Bank
end test

test -f /dev/null convert test/regress/csv-debit-cost.csv --account Assets:Bank
2024/01/01 * Broker
    Expenses:Unknown                         -10 EUR @@ $12
    Assets:Bank                                  $12
end test

test -f /dev/null convert test/regress/csv-debit-cost.csv --account Assets:Bank --invert
2024/01/01 * Broker
    Expenses:Unknown                          10 EUR @@ $12
    Assets:Bank                                 $-12
end test
